WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.652
Opioid crisis accountability.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.772
The other death pipeline.

00:00:04.772 --> 00:00:10.534
Over forty-seven thousand Canadians have died from opioid toxicity since 2016.

00:00:10.534 --> 00:00:14.227
Twenty-two people die every day from toxic drug supply.

00:00:14.227 --> 00:00:16.954
Health Canada approved OxyContin.

00:00:16.954 --> 00:00:20.590
The PMPRB didn't control opioid pricing.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:23.693
Purdue Pharma settled but the death count continues.

00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:29.704
The opioid crisis is the parallel death pipeline to MAID: both are products of institutional failure.

00:00:29.704 --> 00:00:33.409
MAID kills through lethal injection administered by doctors.

00:00:33.409 --> 00:00:37.681
Opioids kill through toxic supply enabled by regulatory failure.

00:00:37.681 --> 00:00:45.329
Both pipelines feed from the same healthcare system that cannot provide adequate pain management, mental health treatment, or addiction services.

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:48.920
Both produce deaths that reduce healthcare system costs.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.988
The institutional architecture is the same.

00:00:51.988 --> 00:00:54.011
Two death pipelines.

00:00:54.011 --> 00:00:56.352
One institutional failure.
